{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"final-frozen-narwhale","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# DC comment/description length convergence — and settle whether the comment limit is even right\n\n## Context\n\nCloud has two convergence suites that exist because oversized text caused real incidents:\n`mutate/test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py` and\n`mutate/test_outbound_description_length_convergence.py`. Each drives an oversized value through\nTWO passes and asserts the second plans nothing — i.e. the truncated value the adapter sent must\ncompare equal to what it reads back, or the differ re-emits the change forever.\n\n**DC has neither.** And DC's comment path is the one with a question mark over it.\n\n## The specific doubt, verified in source\n\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:146-149`:\n\n    def sanitize_comment(self, body: str) -> str:\n        return _shared_sanitize_comment(\n            body, truncate=self._codec.fit_outbound, max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT\n        )\n\nTwo things are bound to the DESCRIPTION here: the limit (`WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT`, 32767) and the\nfitter (`fit_outbound`, the description fit). Cloud does not do this — it routes comments through a\ndedicated `adapters/jira/comment_limits` module, which exists precisely because comment limits are\nnot description limits.\n\n**Whether 32767 is the correct comment ceiling on Data Center is UNKNOWN and currently untestable\nfrom this repo.** That is the substance of this story: not \"port two tests\" but \"find out, then pin\nit\". If the real ceiling is lower, DC silently sends comments Jira rejects or truncates\nserver-side, and the differ never converges.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Establish the real DC comment ceiling. Prefer measurement over documentation: the live harness\n   can post progressively larger comments and observe where the instance refuses. Record the\n   measurement and its date on this ticket — and if it cannot be established, say so and mark the\n   32767 binding UNVERIFIED in the code rather than leaving it looking deliberate.\n2. Port both convergence suites to DC, parametrized alongside Cloud's where the mechanism is\n   shared.\n3. Only then decide whether `max_chars` needs changing. A limit change is a BEHAVIOUR change and,\n   if needed, belongs in its own bug ticket linked from here — not folded into a test story.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/` — DC convergence coverage for comment and description.\n- possibly one live cell to measure the ceiling.\n- NOT the limit change itself (see above).\n\n## Testing\n\nConvergence is a two-pass property, so a single-pass assertion cannot establish it: the test must\nrun the pass twice and assert the SECOND plans nothing for that ticket. A first-pass-only test\nwould pass against a bridge that re-sends the same truncated comment forever.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] an oversized DC comment converges: pass 2 plans no further change for that ticket, asserted\n      on the plan rather than on the absence of an exception\n- [ ] an oversized DC description converges, same assertion shape\n- [ ] the truncation suffix marker survives the round trip and is asserted, not just the length\n- [ ] the real DC comment ceiling is either MEASURED (with the measurement recorded here) or\n      explicitly marked UNVERIFIED at the `max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` binding, so a reader\n      cannot mistake an assumption for a decision\n- [ ] if the ceiling turns out to be wrong, a linked bug is filed and this story does NOT silently\n      change the limit\n- [ ] mutation-checked: raising the limit above the real ceiling, or removing the fit, turns a\n      convergence test red","id":"79d5-63c7-5f31-48a7","parent_id":"3e73-72b5-cff2-40f0","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"DC length convergence, and settle the comment ceiling"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785642270571059001,"uuid":"b3f3f01c-e9f3-47a9-9a59-2eb3a1ca4ef9"}